package murmur:conversation@0.1.0;

/// Host-provided capability available to any hook component.
///
/// The runtime keeps one durable record per conversation: every message it puts
/// in front of the model, in the order it entered the context, appended as one
/// JSON object per line under `~/.murmur/conversations/`. A hook that imports
/// this interface reads that record without holding a `filesystem` grant and
/// without any other artifact being involved. Nothing is ever preopened under
/// `conversations/`; this interface is the only way in.
///
/// Reading is gated by `capabilities.conversation.read: true` on that hook's
/// entry in the capsule operator's own `murmur.yaml`. Default-deny: an ungranted
/// hook still links and still runs, and `read-messages` returns
/// `not-granted`.
///
/// The runtime is the only writer, permanently — there is no write function.
///
/// # What is in scope
///
/// A read answers about the record of the conversation the current task belongs
/// to, which is the record `context-id` on `task-start-event` names. Before the
/// first task of a launch, and for a capsule running `inference.transport:
/// process` (whose CLI owns its own conversation and puts no message list in
/// front of the model), there is no record: the read succeeds with an empty
/// page rather than failing.
///
/// # The tool-message envelope
///
/// A message whose `role` is `"tool"` reaches a reader with its `content`
/// replaced by a JSON object, because a tool result carries more than the one
/// string `content` has room for. Its keys:
///
/// - `__murmur_tool_msg__`: always `true`, and what marks the envelope.
/// - `tool_call_id`: the call this result answers, or `null`.
/// - `is_error`: whether that call failed, or `null`.
/// - `body`: the tool result as the runtime holds it.
///
/// A reader has to handle it. It is the same encoding a compaction hook already
/// receives on `murmur:hook/lifecycle`'s `message` — one encoding, in both
/// places.
interface read {
    use murmur:hook/lifecycle@0.8.0.{message};

    /// One page of the record, newest first.
    record message-page {
        /// The page's messages, `messages[0]` being the most recently appended
        /// one. Every message carries the `id` its record line holds, and a
        /// message whose `role` is `"tool"` carries the envelope this
        /// interface's doc describes.
        messages: list<message>,
        /// Cursor for the next (older) page, or `none` when this page reached
        /// the oldest message in the record.
        next-cursor: option<string>,
        /// Parseable messages in the whole record at the moment this page was
        /// served, not in this page. A record line that is not a message is
        /// skipped and not counted.
        ///
        /// A snapshot, not a loop invariant: the runtime appends while a hook
        /// pages, so `total` can grow from one page to the next and the page
        /// lengths of a completed walk can sum to less than the final `total`.
        /// Every page is a consistent snapshot anchored at the oldest message,
        /// so nothing repeats and nothing is skipped; a loop is sized by
        /// `next-cursor: none`, which is the only correct termination
        /// condition.
        total: u32,
    }

    /// Newest first. `limit` is clamped by the host.
    ///
    /// `cursor` is an opaque token: pass `none` to start at the newest message,
    /// then pass back the `next-cursor` of the page you just read, unmodified.
    /// A cursor naming a position outside the record is `invalid-cursor`.
    /// `limit` is clamped to `1..=100`, so `0` reads one message and a paging
    /// loop always makes progress.
    ///
    /// Errors are strings: `not-granted` when this hook's entry does not declare
    /// `capabilities.conversation.read: true`, `invalid-cursor`, and
    /// `unavailable: <reason>` when the record exists but could not be read. No
    /// record at all is not an error — it is an empty page with `total: 0`.
    read-messages: func(cursor: option<string>, limit: u32) -> result<message-page, string>;
}
